\subsubsection{Ten-seed OU-family and adaptation study}
\IfFileExists{w3d-ou-validation-results-publication.tex-part}{%
  \input{w3d-ou-validation-results-publication.tex-part}%
}{%
  \input{w3d-ou-validation-results-generated.tex-part}%
}

\IfFileExists{ou_validation_vertical.svg}{%
  \begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
    \includesvg[width=\columnwidth,inkscapelatex=false]{ou_validation_vertical}
    \caption{Ten-seed normalized vertical RMS; paired-bootstrap 95\% intervals.}
    \label{fig:ou_mc_vertical}
  \end{figure}
}{}

Across the four stationary JONSWAP seas, seed-wise mean normalized vertical
error is
$\OUValidationOUIIINormalizedMean\pm\OUValidationOUIIINormalizedStd\%$ of
$H_s$ for OU--III and
$\OUValidationOUIINormalizedMean\pm\OUValidationOUIINormalizedStd\%$ for
OU--II.  The paired difference is
$\OUValidationNormalizedDifference$ percentage points with bootstrap 95\%
interval
$[\OUValidationNormalizedDifferenceLow,\OUValidationNormalizedDifferenceHigh]$;
the exact paired sign-flip check gives
$p=\OUValidationNormalizedRandomizationP$.  The ten pairs establish
repeatability within the stated simulator and sensor model, not uncertainty over
alternative simulators, vessels, spectra, or cadences.

\paragraph{Vertical and three-dimensional displacement}
OU--III has lower vertical error in every stationary JONSWAP sea and the
controlled transition.  Table~\ref{tab:ou_mc_axes} also shows lower paired mean
3-D RMS in all \OUValidationThreeDScenarios{} JONSWAP-plus-transition scenarios,
with bootstrap intervals excluding zero.  This does not imply universal
horizontal-axis tuning because the $X/Y$ decomposition is heading dependent and
the directional ensemble is limited.

\paragraph{Second spectrum}
The paired PM--Stokes ensemble reproduces the vertical advantage, with seed-level
mean difference $\OUValidationPMStokesDifference$ percentage points
($[\OUValidationPMStokesDifferenceLow,\OUValidationPMStokesDifferenceHigh]$).
It remains separate rather than pooled into the JONSWAP primary endpoint.

\paragraph{Adaptation mechanism}
Figure~\ref{fig:ou_mc_vertical} shows that online tuning reduces the penalty of
using one FixedNominal point off nominal.  At $H_s=\SI{1.5}{m}$, where
FixedNominal and FixedOracle coincide, OU--III Adaptive is slightly worse by
$\OUValidationIIIHsOneFiveNominalDifference$ percentage points
($[\OUValidationIIIHsOneFiveNominalLow,\OUValidationIIIHsOneFiveNominalHigh]$),
consistent with estimating the operating point from noisy signals.

Channel ablation identifies the mechanism: adaptive $r_S$ with frozen
$(\tau,\sigma_{aw})$ closely tracks full adaptation, whereas adapting the OU
parameters with fixed $r_S$ recovers essentially none of the off-nominal
vertical improvement (Table~\ref{tab:ou_mc_channels}).  The evidence therefore
supports adaptive integral-state regularization as the dominant channel in these
tests; direct OU-parameter adaptation contributes little once $r_S$ is fixed.

\paragraph{Controlled transition and covariance control}
The whole-window transition percentages use the final $H_s=\SI{4.0}{m}$ and are
descriptive.  Table~\ref{tab:ou_transition_segments} shows that Adaptive matches
the nominal fixed point before the transition and gains as the longer-period sea
emerges.  It also beats endpoint-calibrated FixedOracle over the whole record
because that reference is mismatched before the endpoint.

The covariance-maintenance control changes normalized vertical error by at most
$\OUValidationCovSyncWorstDifference$ percentage points
($[\OUValidationCovSyncWorstLow,\OUValidationCovSyncWorstHigh]$), indicating
that periodic latent-acceleration covariance re-alignment is not the source of
the adaptation differences.

